== Public concept versus BITwiki application ==

The wave keeps general knowledge distinct from repository architecture.

<pre>
Ontology        ≠ BITwiki:Ontology
Entity          ≠ BITwiki:Entity types
Relation        ≠ BITwiki:Relationships
Property        ≠ BITwiki:Semantic properties
Category        ≠ MediaWiki Category:
Concept         ≠ MediaWiki Concept:
Schema          ≠ bitwiki-runtime-schema.json
Knowledge graph ≠ BITwiki staging graph
</pre>

Public pages define the general concept first. BITwiki appears only in explicitly labeled application sections.

== Philosophy / information-science ontology audit ==

[[Ontology]] preserves both major senses:

# philosophy — what exists, ontological commitment, general kinds/relations;
# information science — explicit/formal specification of a conceptualization/domain vocabulary.

The article does not reduce philosophical ontology to OWL and does not present a formal ontology artifact as proof of metaphysical truth.

== Concept / entity / category audit ==

[[Concept]], [[Entity]] and [[Category]] remain distinct.

* concept — unit/constituent of thought or structured meaning;
* entity — distinct object of reference/representation;
* category — grouping/kind.

The pages explicitly distinguish a concept such as Planet from an entity such as Mars and from a formal class used to classify individuals.

== Classification / taxonomy audit ==

[[Classification]] is the assignment process.

[[Taxonomy]] is the organized concept/category structure.

The articles also preserve:

<pre>
SKOS broader/narrower ≠ automatically OWL subclass
statistical classification ≠ ontology classification
</pre>

No reasoning rule is derived merely from visual hierarchy.

== Relation / property audit ==

[[Relation]] is the broad connection concept.

[[Property]] covers philosophical attributes and formal predicate/property uses.

RDF property is explicitly presented as a specialized binary relation role rather than the universal meaning of property.

RDFS domain/range are correctly treated as inferential semantics, not ordinary closed-world validation constraints.

== Knowledge-representation audit ==

[[Knowledge representation]] preserves the five-role Davis/Shrobe/Szolovits framing:

* surrogate;
* ontological commitments;
* reasoning theory;
* efficient computation;
* human expression.

The wave does not equate databases, learned embeddings, symbolic KR or knowledge graphs.

[[Knowledge graph]] distinguishes:

* graph database;
* ontology;
* RDF graph;
* symbolic entailment;
* learned link prediction.

Predicted edges are not described as logically entailed facts.

== Schema / metadata audit ==

[[Schema]] separates:

* structural schemas;
* database schemas;
* validation shapes;
* RDFS semantic vocabulary.

[[Metadata]] remains broader than "data about data" and includes descriptive, structural, administrative, preservation and provenance functions.

Schema validity and metadata presence are not treated as truth guarantees.

== Semantic Web standards audit ==

[[Semantic Web]] separates RDF, RDFS, OWL, SPARQL, SKOS and Linked Data.

Machine-readable does not become "human-like machine understanding."

Decentralized identifier use does not eliminate entity-resolution or provenance problems.

== RDF current-state audit ==

As of the Wave 2 audit in September 2026:

* RDF 1.2 Concepts and Abstract Data Model — Candidate Recommendation Snapshot dated 7 April 2026;
* RDF 1.2 Semantics — Candidate Recommendation Snapshot dated 7 April 2026;
* RDF 1.2 Primer — Draft Note dated 26 August 2026;
* several RDF 1.2 serialization/query specifications continue as drafts.

Therefore [[Resource Description Framework]] correctly presents stable RDF concepts version-neutrally and labels RDF 1.2 as an active Candidate Recommendation rather than a finalized Recommendation.

The article also preserves:

<pre>
RDF data model ≠ serialization
RDF graph ≠ RDF dataset
RDF ≠ RDFS
RDF ≠ OWL
open world ≠ missing-is-false
</pre>

== OWL / description logic audit ==

[[Web Ontology Language]] preserves:

* classes vs individuals;
* object vs data properties;
* open-world assumption;
* no general Unique Name Assumption;
* strong <code>owl:sameAs</code> identity;
* OWL profiles;
* relation to description logics.

[[Description logic]] preserves the concepts/roles/individuals model, TBox/ABox distinction, satisfiability, subsumption, instance checking and expressivity/decidability trade-off.

OWL is not presented as identical to description logic as a whole.

== Inference audit ==

[[Inference]] separates:

* deduction;
* induction;
* abduction;
* probabilistic inference;
* causal inference;
* semantic entailment;
* algorithmic inference procedure.

Soundness and completeness are defined separately.

RDF/OWL derived assertions are separated from statistically predicted graph edges.

